  translation: "1. Arjuna said: You praise the renunciation of actions, O Krishna, and again you praise yoga. Tell me which one of these two is the better, the one you hold as settled.\n2. The blessed Lord said: Renunciation and the yoga of action both bring about release. But of these two, the yoga of action excels the mere renunciation of action.\n3. He who does not hate and does not long is to be known as a constant renouncer. For, free of the pairs, O mighty-armed, he is released from bondage without strain.\n4. The childish say that the path of knowledge and yoga are different, with opposed fruits. The wise do not. He who has rightly practiced even one of them obtains the fruit of both.\n5. The place that the men of knowledge reach is reached by the yogins too. He who sees the path of knowledge and yoga as one sees rightly.\n6. But renunciation, O mighty-armed, is hard to reach without yoga. The sage who is yoked to yoga reaches before long the highest renunciation, steadfastness in knowledge of the self.\n7. Yoked to yoga, his mind pure, his body and senses mastered, his self the self of all beings, he is not stained even while he acts.\n8. \"I do nothing at all\": so the steadied man who knows the truth should think, seeing, hearing, touching, smelling, eating, going, sleeping, breathing,\n9. speaking, letting out, taking, even opening and closing the eyes, holding that the senses move among their objects.\n10. He who acts having laid his actions on the Lord and given up attachment is not stained by evil, as a lotus leaf by water.\n11. With the body, the mind, and the intellect, and with the senses too, alone, yogins do action, having given up attachment, for the cleansing of the self.\n12. The steadied man, having given up the fruit of action, reaches the peace that belongs to steadfastness. The unsteadied man, driven by desire and clinging to the fruit, is bound.\n13. Having renounced all actions with the mind, the self-controlled embodied one rests easily in the nine-gated city, not doing at all, and not causing action.\n14. The Lord creates neither agency nor the things people produce, nor the joining of an action to its fruit. One's own nature, though, goes on.\n15. The all-pervading one takes no one's evil, nor their good work either. Knowledge is covered by ignorance, and by that creatures are confused.\n16. But for those in whom that ignorance of the self has been destroyed by knowledge, knowledge illumines the supreme reality, as the sun illumines every form.\n17. Those whose understanding is set on that, whose self is that, who stand in that, and for whom that is the highest course, go to what does not come back, their stain shaken off by knowledge.\n18. The wise see the same in a brāhmaṇa endowed with learning and humility, in a cow, in an elephant, in a dog, and in one who cooks dogs.\n19. Right here, birth is conquered by those whose mind stands still in sameness. For Brahman is flawless and the same, and so they abide in Brahman.\n20. He should not exult on gaining what is dear, nor be shaken on gaining what is unwelcome. With his understanding firm and free of bewilderment, the knower of Brahman abides in Brahman.\n21. He whose mind is unattached amid external contacts finds the joy that is in the self. With his mind held in the yoga of Brahman, he reaches a joy that does not fail.\n22. For the enjoyments born of contact are nothing but sources of pain. They have a beginning and an end, O son of Kuntī, and the wise man does not delight in them.\n23. He who can endure, right here, the surge born of desire and anger, until he is released from the body: he is a yogin, and he is a happy man.\n24. The yogin whose joy is within, whose delight is within, and whose light is within alone, reaches the peace of Brahman, having become Brahman while still alive.\n25. The seers whose stain is worn away, whose doubt is cut, whose senses are held in, and who are intent on the good of every being, gain the peace of Brahman.\n26. For renunciates freed from desire and anger, their minds held in, who know the self, the peace of Brahman lies on both sides: while they live, and when they are dead.\n27. Having put the contacts outside, and the eye between the brows, and having made the out-breath and the in-breath even as they move inside the nostrils,\n28. the sage whose senses, mind, and understanding are held in, intent on release, from whom desire, fear, and anger have gone — he who lives so is free already.\n29. Having known me as the enjoyer of sacrifices and austerities, the great lord of all worlds, and the friend of all beings, he reaches peace.",
